  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal zone stalled just south of the area will \nbring another period of showers and isolated thunderstorms, mainly \nlate tonight through the first half of Thursday. \n\nAn east-west oriented frontal zone will continue to sag slowly \nsouthward towards the NY/PA border through this evening. Weak \nconvergence along the frontal zone will continue to produce a few \nscattered showers or patchy drizzle through this evening, mainly \nacross the Southern Tier and Finger Lakes. \n\nOvernight, a weak baroclinic wave will run east along the stalled \nfrontal zone, forcing the front to lift back north into southern \nportions of Western and Central NY. The differential temperature \nadvection pattern forced by the frontal wave will tighten the \nthermal structure across the boundary, with associated frontogenesis \nand deformation supporting an area of showers moving from southwest \nto northeast across the area late tonight through the first half of \nThursday. There may be just enough elevated instability to support a \nfew isolated thunderstorms, mainly across the Southern Tier and \nFinger Lakes.\n\nPWAT values will rise to near 2.0\" along and south of the stalled \nboundary, and the frontal zone laying out parallel to the mid level \nflow also fits the conceptual model for locally heavy rainfall from \ntraining heavy showers. Most of the recent CAMS guidance suggests \nthis potential is relatively higher across Ohio and PA, with a lower \nrisk extending into the Southern Tier and Finger Lakes.\n\nThe showers will gradually end from west to east Thursday afternoon \nand evening, with mainly dry weather returning to Western NY by \nearly evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief period of drier, cooler conditions is \nexpected Thursday night through Saturday morning. \n\nThe frontal zone will accelerate southeast and away from the region \nThursday night as high pressure builds southeast across Ontario and \nQuebec. This high will bring a return to dry weather Thursday night \nthrough Saturday morning. Modest cold advection behind the front \nwill bring slightly below average temperatures to close out the \nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another period of unsettled weather develops late \nSaturday and continues through early next week.\n\nAnother Pacific origin shortwave will round the top of the large \nridge of the south central US over the weekend, with increasing \nforcing and moisture transport spreading east across the Great Lakes \nregion. Based on latest model timing, first rain chances will arrive \nin Western NY by Saturday afternoon, then spread east across the \nremainder of the area Saturday night.\n\nSeveral waves of low pressure are then forecast to move east over \nthe southern Great Lakes region through early next week. Model and \nensemble guidance begins to diverge on the placement and timing of \nthe frontal zone and various waves of low pressure by Sunday and \nMonday. The pattern will need to be monitored for heavy rain risk, \ngiven the elongated east-west frontal zone laying out parallel to \nthe mid level flow with ample moisture poised just south of the \nboundary.",
